---
description: Keep shared visual chrome consistent between director and public shells
alwaysApply: true
---

# Director / public chrome consistency

When you change **shared visual chrome** (placement, grouping, density,
disclosure, banners, menu footers, nav patterns), do it on **both** sides in
the same change unless the feature is truly one-audience.

## Applies to

- Menu / rail / hamburger structure (where sections live, dividers, what sits
  at the bottom)
- Cross-cutting banners and roll-ups (What's New, status strips, update toasts)
- Collapsible vs always-open nav groups
- Shared controls that both audiences use (theme, language, text size) when
  the interaction pattern itself changes

## Does not force mirroring

- Role-only tools (Take Roll, Directors admin, lesson log)
- Public-only destinations (student lookup, subscribe)
- Copy that must differ by audience (staff vs family wording)
- Data privacy boundaries (staff-only collections stay staff-only)

## How to ship

1. Touch both shells in one commit when the chrome is shared
   (`src/director/DirectorApp.tsx` + `src/public/PublicLayout.tsx`, or the
   shared component both import).
2. If you only fix one side first, finish the other before calling the work
   done. Do not leave "public has it, director still has the old place."
3. Prefer one shared component over two copies when the UI is the same
   (example: `WhatsNewBanner`).

## Example (2026-08-29)

What's New moved to the **bottom of the menu** on public. Directors still
saw it on Today until the rail/menu got the same placement. That split is
exactly what this rule forbids.
